  "requirement": "`AgentSettings<D>` identifies the configurations a domain can use in its cache.\nTBV implements this sealed trait for `AgentConfig` and `AgentWithTools<D>`.\n\n```rust,ignore\npub trait AgentSettings<D: Domain>: Settings<D> { }\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The structural obligations are: (1) the trait `AgentSettings<D: Domain>` has the supertrait `Settings<D>`, (2) it is sealed, and (3) it is implemented for `AgentConfig` and `AgentWithTools<D>`. An alternative with an unrelated supertrait, an unsealed public trait, or an impl for only one of the two types would violate the text, so each is a real obligation. The supplied roots cover all three.\n\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/config.rs#trait[AgentSettings]` declares `pub trait AgentSettings<D: Domain>: sealed::Settings<D> {}`. Its supertrait lives in the `pub(crate) mod sealed` in `domain_tools.rs`, so outside crates cannot implement it. Its doc test is `compile_fail` for a third-party impl.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/config.rs#impl[AgentConfig/AgentSettings<D>]` and `#impl[AgentWithTools<D>/AgentSettings<D>]` supply the two required impls.\n- The sealed `Settings<D>` trait and its impls for `AgentConfig` and `AgentWithTools<D>` are in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/domain_tools.rs#mod[sealed]`. A reviewer reaches them by following the supertrait, so they need no separate marker.\n\nNo further roots are missing.\n\nbehavioral: The requirement has three checkable obligations. The first is that `AgentSettings<D>` is a trait that identifies the configurations a domain can use in its cache. The second is that it is sealed. The third is that it is implemented for `AgentConfig` and `AgentWithTools<D>`.\n\n- **Sealed:** The `compile_fail` doctest on `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/config.rs#trait[AgentSettings]` tries a third-party `impl<D: Domain> AgentSettings<D> for ThirdPartyConfig {}` and expects compilation to fail. The doctest imports `trust_but_verify::agent::AgentSettings` and `trust_but_verify::domain::Domain`. The search shows `AgentSettings` is reachable from `crate::agent`, so the failure should come from the `sealed::Settings` supertrait and not from a bad import. The trait carries a {test} reference to the requirement.\n- **Impl for `AgentConfig`:** `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/configured.rs#mod[tests]/fn[bedrock_configuration_attaches_to_a_cache_without_being_persisted]` passes an `AgentConfig::Bedrock` value to `ValidationCache::with_agent`, which takes `impl AgentSettings<D>`. It asserts that the cache holds that backend and that the validator runs. This test would not compile without the impl.\n- **Impl for `AgentWithTools<D>` and use in a cache:** `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/configured.rs#mod[tests]/fn[registered_tools_use_the_current_context_and_ordinary_node_ids]` passes `backend.with_tool(...)`, an `AgentWithTools<D>`, to `with_agent`. It then asks a question through the cache and asserts the tool lists, tool calls and usage stats.\n\nBoth tests carry {test} references to the subject. No other behavioral obligation exists. The impl blocks are empty marker impls with no conditionals.\n\nNo gaps.",
